EXT. DRY CREEK BED - SUNSET

Cracked clay. A dead cottonwood. Four riders have dismounted.

MARSHAL JEB TRASK, 50s. Gray mustache. Badge pinned crooked.

He watches.

Across the dry bed, ALMA SOTELO, 30s. Rifle across her saddle. Black hat.

Beside her, her brother TOMASO, 20s. Hand on his holster. Sweat.

Behind them, DEPUTY HOLLIS FINCH, 19. Shotgun. Too big for him.

Nobody speaks.

Wind. A tumbleweed rolls through, stops against Trask's boot.

TRASK
Alma.

ALMA
Marshal.

TRASK
Long ride.

ALMA
Longer back.

Tomaso laughs, high and nervous.

TOMASO
Don't talk to him, Alma.

ALMA
Be quiet, Tomaso.

TRASK
You've got the mule.

ALMA
It's my mule.

TRASK
It was Dalton's.

Alma shrugs. Hollis licks his lips.

HOLLIS
Marshal, I got a bad feeling.

TRASK
Everybody has.

ALMA
(simultaneously)
Everybody has.

^TOMASO
Everybody has.

Silence. Trask almost smiles.

TRASK
Alma. Put the rifle down.

ALMA
You first.

TRASK
Hollis.

HOLLIS
Sir?

TRASK
Put yours down.

HOLLIS
Sir?

TRASK
Do it.

Hollis lowers the shotgun. Trask unbuckles his gun belt, drops it. Alma watches. Slowly she slides the rifle from the saddle and lays it flat in the dust.

Tomaso does not move.

ALMA
Tomaso.

TOMASO
They'll hang us.

ALMA
They'll hang you. Not me.

She turns her back on the marshal. Walks to Tomaso. Takes his gun hand in both of hers. Opens the fingers one at a time. The pistol falls.

Trask exhales. Hollis sits down on the clay. The tumbleweed rolls on.
